  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nCurrently storms continue to move through northeast TN and southwest \nVA as we remain under a strong ridge over most of the United States. \nWeak disturbance is moving through, but it's just enough to help \norganize convection across the area. Atmosphere out there \n(especially across northeast TN and southwest VA) is capable of \nproducing strong downbursts from collapsing storms and small hail. \nWe could also see some minor flooding as these storms continue to \nrun over the same counties today. But at this time widespread severe \nweather and/or widespread flooding is not anticipated on this side \nof the Appalachian Mountains.\n\nFor Monday, an inverted surface trough across the Plateau and \nwestern Tennessee valley will support isolated convection mainly \nalong and west of interstate 40. Again, strong/gusty winds and \nfrequent lightning will be the main concern.\n\nFor Tuesday and Wednesday, upper ridging builds back over the \nTennessee valley and southern Appalachians producing hot and dry \nconditions. Dewpoints will be in the middle and upper 60s which will \nlimit the heat indices below heat advisory criteria.\n\nThe upper ridge flattens for Thursday as a short-waves and frontal \nboundary approaches the area. Scattered showers and storms are \nexpected. The boundary moves south of the area for Friday.\n\nFor Saturday, return southerly flow around surface ridge east of the \nAppalachians will bring moisture and instability back into the area.\n\nIn the upper levels, the ridge builds back into the region by Monday \nafternoon although centered near Oklahoma. Monday will have cooler \ntemps (although still very warm) with highs in the Tennessee Valley \nmainly in the upper 80s. Showers and storms will be isolated to \nscattered Monday afternoon as an inverted surface trough forms over \nKentucky and Tennessee.",
